@page "/components/ai/datatable"
@implements IAsyncDisposable

<PageOutlet Url="components/ai/datatable"
            Title="AiDataTable"
            Description="BitAiDataTable renders a JSON array of objects as a table - one row per object, one column per key - and keeps every column while a model is still streaming the rows." />

<DemoPage Name="AiDataTable"
          Description="BitAiDataTable renders a JSON array of objects as a table: one row per object and one column per key, headed by the key written as a label. The columns are the union of the keys of every row, in the order they first appear, so a column a later row brings is never dropped - which is what a table filling in from a model's partial JSON needs. A nested value is shown as its JSON text and a missing one as an empty cell, and a wide table scrolls sideways in its own box rather than widening the conversation around it."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubAIUrl="DataTable/BitAiDataTable.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="AI/DataTable/BitAiDataTableDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.AI" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            BitAiDataTable needs nothing but its <b>Rows</b>, a <b>JsonElement</b>. It is the table
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/structureddataview">BitAiStructuredDataView</BitLink> and
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/artifactview">BitAiArtifactView</BitLink> draw an array of objects with, and the one
            to render a tool result or any parsed JSON with yourself. It is a read-only view: the rows are not sorted, filtered or
            paged - reach for BitDataGrid of Bit.BlazorUI.Extras for that. The live demo of streaming rows is the
            <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/structured">Structured output</BitLink> scenario's too.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            The box a wide table scrolls in sideways is a group in the tab order, so the keyboard can reach it and scroll it
            in every engine (WebKit makes no scroller focusable on its own), named by its <b>AriaLabel</b> or the Table
            string. It is a group rather than a region, so the tables of a page or a chat do not make a run of landmarks.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                Hand <b>Rows</b> a JSON array of objects: every object is a row and every key a column, headed by the key
                written as a label (<b>contextWindow</b> becomes "Context Window").
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiDataTable Rows="models" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Columns & cells" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                The columns are every key any row has, in the order they first appear - the second row brings <b>tags</b>, the
                third <b>owner</b>, and both columns stay. A row without a key leaves its cell empty, as does a null; a nested
                object or array is written as its JSON text; numbers and booleans as they were written (<b>true</b>, never
                True). A header splits a key at its capitals and its underscores: <b>lastLogin</b> reads "Last Login" and
                <b>time_zone</b> "Time zone".
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiDataTable Rows="mixed" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Streaming rows" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example3CodeFiles" Id="example3">
            <div>
                A model streams its rows as JSON text that is incomplete for most of the answer. The session gathers it in a
                <b>BitAiStructuredDataPart</b>, whose <b>Value</b> is a lenient parse of what has arrived, so the table fills in
                row by row and cell by cell while the JSON is still open. The transport here is a scripted one: no model, no key.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton OnClick="AskForOptionsAsync" Disabled="optionsSession.IsGenerating">Compare the options</BitButton>
            <br /><br />
            @if (OptionsPart?.Value is { } optionRows)
            {
                <BitAiDataTable Rows="optionRows" />
            }
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Wide tables" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                A table wider than the room it has scrolls sideways in its own box rather than widening the page or the
                conversation around it. While it overflows, the box is a group in the tab order - Tab to it and scroll it with the
                arrow keys - named by the <b>AriaLabel</b> of the table, or the Table text of the strings in force when it has none.
                A <b>tabindex</b> written on the table (an attribute, passed through) decides that for it instead.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiDataTable Rows="benchmarks" AriaLabel="Benchmark results" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                <b>BitParams</b> with a <b>BitAiDataTableParams</b> sets the <b>Classes</b> and <b>Styles</b> of the parts - and the
                parameters every component shares, such as <b>Dir</b> or <b>AriaLabel</b> - of every table inside it, rather than
                the rows a single table shows. A table keeps what it sets itself: the second one gives <b>Styles</b> of its own,
                so the cascaded ones do not reach it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitParams Parameters="dataTableParams">
                <BitAiDataTable Rows="models" />
                <br />
                <BitAiDataTable Rows="models" Styles="@(new() { Cell = "font-style: italic" })" />
            </BitParams>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> reach the root - the box the table scrolls in - and <b>Styles</b> and
                <b>Classes</b> each part. The public <b>--bit-AiDataTable-*</b> variables (see the CSS variables table)
                inherit, so set on <b>:root</b>, a container or a table's <b>Style</b> they re-skin every table below.
            </div>
            <br />
            @* A plain element of this page for the scoped ::deep rules of the custom classes to anchor on. *@
            <div>
                <BitAiDataTable Rows="models"
                                Style="border-radius: 1rem; border-color: #8a2be2"
                                Styles="@(new() { HeaderCell = "background-color: #8a2be2; color: white", Cell = "font-style: italic" })" />
                <br />
                <BitAiDataTable Rows="models" Classes="@(new() { Root = "custom-table", Row = "custom-table-row" })" />
                <br />
                <div style="--bit-AiDataTable-header-background: #d2691e; --bit-AiDataTable-header-color: white; --bit-AiDataTable-cell-padding: 0.25rem 0.5rem; --bit-AiDataTable-divider-color: #d2691e">
                    <BitAiDataTable Rows="models" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                In right-to-left text the columns run from the right and every cell starts on its right edge, whether the
                direction comes from <b>Dir</b> or from a <b>dir</b> attribute around the table.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiDataTable Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Rows="persianModels" />
            <br />
            <div dir="rtl">
                <BitAiDataTable Rows="persianModels" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
